## Ammonium chloride (sal ammoniac)
component-ammonium-chloride = Ammonium chloride (sal ammoniac)
    .genitive = ammonium chloride
component-ammonium-chloride-decomposes-into = ammonia and hydrogen chloride (on a cold surface they join again as a white coating)
component-ammonium-chloride-electrolysis-note = Under current, as in salt water, hydrogen forms at the cathode and chlorine at the anode: both from chloride ions and water.
component-ammonium-chloride-description-novice = White crystals that look like salt. Dissolved in water, they make it noticeably colder — that is how first-aid cold packs work.
component-ammonium-chloride-description-school = Dissolving NH₄Cl takes in heat (+14.8 kJ/mol): breaking up the crystal takes more energy than is released as water surrounds the ions. So the solution cools down. On crystallising the heat comes back.
component-ammonium-chloride-safety-school = Harmful if swallowed and irritates the eyes. Wear goggles. When heated it gives off irritating gases.
